High-resolution digital image correlation (HRDIC) has been used to study strain distributions in two magnesium alloys subject to tensile deformation. One alloy, the conventional Mg–Al–Zn alloy AZ31 exhibits a strong basal texture and is known to show poor formability. The other alloy, ZEK100, is a commercial dilute Mg–Zn–Rare-earth (RE) alloy that shows a weaker split basal texture and is known to show improved formability. Both alloys were heat treated to obtain a fully recrystallized grain structure with similar average grain size. This enables the effect of differences in single-crystal properties and texture to be isolated. The following conclusions may be drawn from this work:  When processed to achieve the same microstructural state, ZEK100 has a similar yield strength but lower work hardening rate than AZ31. Both alloys have a uniaxial tensile elongation to failure in excess of 20 pct. HRDIC maps show strong regions of strain localization, particularly at grain boundaries, in both AZ31 and ZEK100. The maximum strain concentration in AZ31 is significantly higher than for ZEK100. Well-defined basal slip bands are observed in both AZ31 and ZEK100. Regions of diffuse slip were also identified and were more common in ZEK100, many of which correspond to non-basal slip, but with limited and ambiguous evidence of  ⟨ c + a ⟩ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
